Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:34:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] mb/radxa/x4: Restart the RP2040 and hide it from the
 boot options

The RP2040's RUN pin follows PLTRST#. A reset supervisor (U42, SGM811)
holds its BOOTSEL line low for up to 840 ms after the RP2040's 3.3 V
rail rises. When PLTRST# deasserts first, the RP2040 starts its USB
bootloader instead of its own firmware and shows up as a USB drive.

Late in ramstage, on normal boot and on S3 resume, wait until 1 s
after reset, then pulse RST_RP2040 (GPP_B17) for 100 us to restart
it. Q29 pulls RUN low against the push-pull PLTRST# buffer behind
33 ohms, so the pulse is kept short.

Both control pads were native ISH I2C pins, which nothing on the
board uses. Make GPP_B17 a GPIO output, low by default, and
RECOVERY_RP2040 (GPP_B7) a GPIO input, so firmware never pulls
BOOTSEL low. GPP_B7 uses PAD_RESET(PWROK), the stock reset config;
RSMRST, which it used before, can't be mapped on these communities.

In its ROM bootloader the RP2040 on USB2 port 6 still shows up as an
edk2 boot option, and booting it stalls until the user picks another.
Hide the port from the edk2 boot options; the device stays usable from
the shell and the OS.

